is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is pc_0_0’s host routing configuration. Its kernel routing table has an incorrect default gateway, causing all off-subnet traffic to be forwarded to a next hop that does not exist on the LAN.

Mechanism: pc_0_0 can reach on-link destinations (10.0.0.1) via ARP and L2, but any remote network (e.g., 10.0.1.0/24 or fabric loopbacks) requires a default route. pc_0_0’s default route points to 10.0.0.254, which is not present on the 10.0.0.0/24 segment, so ARP for that next hop fails. Linux then returns “Destination Host Unreachable” from 10.0.0.2 for those probes. This exactly matches the symptom: local leaf still reachable, everything else not.

Localization: On pc_0_0, ip route shows “default via 10.0.0.254 dev eth0” while the attached leaf’s SVI is 10.0.0.1/24 (leaf_router_0_0 eth2). The neighbor table confirms “10.0.0.254 dev eth0 FAILED” and a valid entry for 10.0.0.1. Pings from pc_0_0 to off-subnet addresses (10.0.1.2, 172.16.0.5) fail with host-unreachable; leaf_router_0_0 can ping pc_0_0, and pc_0_0 can ping 10.0.0.1, proving the L2 link is fine.

Root cause: The default gateway on pc_0_0 was misconfigured to 10.0.0.254 during the maintenance task instead of 10.0.0.1.

Corrective fix: On pc_0_0, replace the default route with “ip route replace default via 10.0.0.1 dev eth0” and update the persistent network config (e.g., /etc/network/interfaces or NetworkManager) to use 10.0.0.1 as the default gateway. After correction, off-subnet connectivity should be restored. 
